      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/10d3d9a3b026fc1511d9e7a57c1f3276606c9397f0045fdb7748e3038a42fb21.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we have that \n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/5dfcb1cff13cc5f5b6dcfa42a7e1d08184e54a7edd91717b795692068db37121.png)\n\nThen, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}$ and ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/3c4b6cd6b5649a18bd768b3086ccef5a995f9745a9ce90160507c9d92f46f8dc.png)\n\nAs a result, ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}$ is the following: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/62373d6926552c34849e91115163dba42483c6259482bc4893704af570749c89.png)\n\nFinally, to get the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}$ or ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/252c206ca03b339c4bb186c95d21cd26110fc902f0ab9bca6df2d8d594aacba5.png)",
